'''addebted†''', ''a.'' [Sc.; at first ''addettit'', ''addetted'', for earlier ''endetted'': see ''indebted''.] Indebted.

'''addecimate†''' (a-des′i-māt), ''v. t.'' [< L. ''addecimatus'', pp. of ''addecimare'', < ''ad'', to, + ''decimare'', take the tenth: see ''decimate''.] To take or ascertain the tithe or tenth part of; tithe; decimate. ''Cockeram.''

'''addeem†''' (a-dēm′), ''v. t.'' [< ''ad-'' + ''deem''. Cf. ''addoom''.] '''1.''' To award; adjudge; sentence.
{{smaller block|Unto him they did ''addeeme'' the prise.
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., V. iii. 15.}}}}
'''2.''' To deem; judge; esteem; account.
{{smaller block|She scorns to be ''addeemed'' so worthless-base.
{{right|''Daniel'', Civil Wars.}}}}

'''addendum''' (a-den′dum), ''n.''; pl. ''addenda'' (-dä̤). [L., gerund of ''addere'', add: see ''add''.] A thing to be added; an addition; an appendix to a work.—'''Addendum-circle''' {{smaller|(of a gear), in ''mach.'', a circle}}

readings.c2s3:
{{smaller|which touches the points of the teeth.—'''Addendum of a tooth,''' in ''mach.'', that part of the tooth of a gear which lies between the pitch-circle and the point.}}

'''adder'''<sup>1</sup> (ad′ėr), ''n.'' [< ME. ''adder'', ''addere'', ''addre'', ''edder'', ''eddre'', etc., forms interchanging with the more correct ''nadder'', ''naddere'', ''naddre'', ''nadre'', ''neddere'', ''neddre'', etc. (through confusion of ''a nadder'' with ''an adder''; cf. ''apron'', ''auger'', ''orange'', ''umpire'', which have lost their initial ''n'' in the same way), < AS. ''nædre'', ''næddre'' = OS. ''nadra'' = D. ''adder'' = OHG. ''natara'', ''natra'', MHG. ''natere'', ''nater'', G. ''natter'' = Icel. ''nathra'', f., ''nathr'', m., = Goth. ''nadrs'' = Ir. ''nathair'' = W. ''neidr'', a snake, a serpent. The L. ''natrix'', a water-snake, is a different word, prop. a swimmer, < ''nare'', swim. The word has no connection with ''atter'', poison, q. v.] '''1.''' The popular English name of the viper, ''Vipera communis'', now ''Pelias berus'', a common venomous serpent of Europe (and the only poisonous British reptile), belonging to the family ''Viperidæ'', of the suborder ''Solenoglypha'', of the order ''Ophidia''. {{smaller|It grows to a length}}
